//? Title: Stochastic Depth: Survival, Scaling and Visible Optimization
//? Subtitle: Tensor computations, explicit checks and reproducible local figures
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Stochastic depth randomly removes a residual contribution while preserving its shortcut.
We check both scaling conventions, whole-batch and per-row masks, endpoint behavior and exact
derivatives, then explicitly compare constant-depth and masked CIFAR-20 fits using tensor Adam.
)
\toc

Source example: `examples/cpp/source/nn/1_convolutional_neural_networks/1.3_residual_and_dense_families/stochastic_depth.cpp`.

> The method

Let b be Bernoulli(p). The original convention uses bF in training and pF in inference;
the inverted convention uses bF/p in training and F in inference. These match expected branch
outputs for p>0, but their fluctuations differ. At p=0 both training conventions give zero;
inverted inference still returns F. Deterministic endpoints avoid division by zero.

$$y=x+mF(x),\qquad J_y=I+mJ_F,\qquad
 p_l=1-\frac{l}{L}(1-p_{\rm last}),\quad l=1,\ldots,L.$$
\alttext(A constant sampled multiplier changes the branch contribution and its derivatives, while the shortcut remains.)

A whole-batch gate is one scalar shared by every image; per-example gates have shape
[N,1,...,1], shared across each image's features. They are nondifferentiable sampled constants.
The visible fixture checks binary gates, outputs, input and learned-weight derivatives for
both conventions and mask granularities at p=0,.6,1. Independent mask counts are 512 batches
or 8192 rows for the interior-probability rate checks; spatial pixels do not count as separate
samples. Inference and endpoint calls do not consume random masks.

The linear schedule has expected active masks L-(1-p_last)(L+1)/2. With L=54 and
p_last=.5 this is 40.25, not 40.5: block indices begin at one. This implementation computes
EVERY branch before masking and updates its BN moments even when dropped. It demonstrates
regularization and does not implement conditional-execution speedup or memory skipping.
A caller-owned generator supplies one stream rather than the source's per-block seeds.

The complete CIFAR 6n+2 classifier uses a 3×3 conv/BN/ReLU stem, three n-unit stages,
two bias-free filters per unit, option-A average-pool/zero-pad shortcuts and post-add ReLU.
Global mean and a dense classifier finish the model. BN learns scale/shift, eps 1e-5;
He-uniform fan-in initialization and this optimizer schedule are adaptations. CIFAR-110
has 1727962 learned/8096 moving scalars and 54 residual blocks; its backward probe retains
all blocks/widths at 16-pixel synthetic inputs. CIFAR-20 has 269722/1376 scalars and nine units.

Two visible fits reset parameter, minibatch, augmentation and mask seeds, comparing final
survival one against .5 with whole-batch inverted DropPath. Both retain all CIFAR-20 layers,
use 128 fit/64 validation/64 official-test images, ten batch-eight epochs (160 updates, kept short for build time) of Adam(.001), explicit
kernel L2=.0001/2 and padded crops/flips. Each selects paired parameters/moving state by
validation NLL before testing. Equal seeds alone do not establish a general accuracy advantage;
this tiny experiment differs from both the original example's larger fit and the paper schedule.

The editable concept diagram is `diagrams/stochastic_depth_concept.tikz`. Regeneration uses
LaTeX with PGF/TikZ and standalone, plus dvisvgm; exports use the saved SVG.

```{bash, results="output", file="figures/stochastic_depth_concept.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/stochastic_depth_concept.tikz > build/stochastic_depth_concept.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/stochastic_depth_concept.svg build/stochastic_depth_concept.dvi > build/stochastic_depth_concept.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered local concept diagram.\n'
```
